# Rust style

Match the existing crate. Edition is 2024: use implicit format captures (`{error}`) and `let`-`else`.

- Take `&str`, `&[T]`, or another reference when the function does not need to own the value. Clone only when the value must outlive the borrow or move to another thread (`Arc::clone`).
- Do not add `unsafe` unless there is no safe alternative. Keep the block small and comment the invariant that makes it sound.
- Public types implement `Debug`. Domain errors stay as `thiserror` enums in `errors.rs`.
- Keep database access in `database` and device polling in `sensor_control`. Do not grow `main` with new domain logic.
- Represent a closed set with an enum, not a free string.
- Prefer iterators over index loops when the index is unused.
- Do not add a dependency for something `std` or an existing crate already does.
- Log lifecycle at `info`, routine I/O at `debug` or `trace`, skipped data at `warn`, and failures at `error`.
- Put unit tests in a `#[cfg(test)]` module in the same file as the code they cover. New behavior gets a test.
